          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I stopped my blood pressure tablets two months ago and my home readings are now around 155/95. Is that an emergency, and what does guidance describe about treatment and follow-up?",
          "answer": "**Is this an emergency?** Based on the description you gave, the symptom check did not flag an emergency. That is not a clearance. The check only covers a fixed list of presentations. If you develop severe or worsening symptoms, or anything new and unexplained, seek medical advice promptly, and seek emergency care if symptoms are severe.\n\n**What the guidance says:** The hypertension summary (General clinical reference, educational summary) explains that high blood pressure usually has no symptoms and is found by measurement. It also notes that blood pressure varies through the day, which is why a diagnosis rests on repeated readings rather than one. Your home readings are a useful reason to talk to a clinician, but the passages I retrieved do not say what a reading of 155/95 means for you.\n\n**What I could not find:** The retrieved material does not cover what happens after stopping blood pressure tablets, how often follow-up should happen, or what treatment options exist. I also cannot tell you whether to restart your tablets or change them. Please contact the clinician who prescribed them, or your usual care team, and tell them what you have stopped and when, and what your home readings have been. Do not restart or change medication based on this answer.\n\nThe diet guidance (Diet for high blood pressure, lifestyle reference) describes sodium, potassium and alcohol as things that affect blood pressure. It also cautions that potassium-based salt substitutes are not right for everyone, including people with kidney disease or on certain medications, so check with your clinician before using one.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
